course: dictionary
lesson: 11420
title: Acoustics: how a room sounds
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Acoustics is a word for how sound behaves in a place. The acoustics of a room are the way sound travels through it and reaches your ears. «The acoustics in the hall were so good that every word the teacher said was clear.» Good acoustics mean sound is clear, and poor acoustics mean it is muddy or hard to hear.
explain: Acoustics can also mean the science of sound: how sound is made, how it travels and how we hear it. In science you learn that sound bounces off hard surfaces, and that bouncing sound is an echo. «The empty gym was so echoey that every clap came back again and again.» The clue is empty and echoey: the bare walls sent the sound back.
explain: Soft things stop sound from bouncing. Curtains and carpets absorb sound, so a room with them is quieter and calmer. That is why halls and theatres are built with great care. «The room had thick curtains and a soft carpet, so the music sounded warm and clear.» The soft things are the clue that the room has good acoustics.
fact: Acoustics means how sound travels and is heard in a room or building.
fact: «Good acoustics» means music and speech sound clear.
fact: «Poor acoustics» means sound is muddy or hard to hear.
fact: Hard surfaces reflect sound, which makes echoes.
fact: Soft surfaces, such as curtains and carpets, absorb sound.
exercise: «The hall had good acoustics, so the choir sounded clear.» What does good acoustics mean? | Nobody is allowed to sing there | The hall is very large and tall | The hall is painted a nice colour | *Sound travels and is heard clearly in the room
exercise: Which surface is most likely to make an echo? | A pile of cushions | *A hard tiled wall | A velvet curtain | A thick, soft carpet
exercise: «The thick curtains and carpet made the room quiet.» What do curtains and carpets do to sound? | *Soak it up so less of it bounces back | Carry it across the street | Make it much louder | Turn it into light
test: What is the science of sound called? | Biology | *Acoustics | Arithmetic | Geography
test: «Poor acoustics made the speech muddy and hard to follow.» What does poor acoustics mean here? | The room is too cold | The room is too bright | Everyone is singing | *Sound is hard to hear clearly
exam: Maya claps in an empty gym and hears echoes. What makes the echoes? | The gym is full of soft mats | *The bare walls and hard floor bounce the sound back | Maya is very quiet | The sound is soaked up by the air
